<TopicPage slug="subjects/earth-science/intermediate/volcanoes-and-igneous-processes">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Volcanoes are <strong>the planet releasing internal heat and building new crust</strong> —
    destructive and constructive at once. Eruption style is predictable from magma chemistry,
    hazards are specific to eruption type, and modern monitoring turns them from catastrophes
    into managed events.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="magma chemistry, eruption styles, hazards, forecasting" widget="equationStep" config={volcCfg} />

  <h2>Volcanoes as global players</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Plate settings predict locations:</strong> subduction zones (the explosive Pacific Ring of Fire — the plate-tectonics lesson), spreading centres (mid-ocean ridges — most volcanism on Earth, underwater), hotspots (Hawaii, Yellowstone — mantle plumes drifting under moving plates).</li>
    <li><strong>Volcanic winters changed history:</strong> Tambora 1815 → "the year without a summer" → crop failures and famine; Pinatubo 1991 cooled global temperature ~0.5 °C for two years — the natural experiment behind solar-geoengineering debates (the environmental-science ladder).</li>
    <li><strong>Igneous rocks are the raw record:</strong> granite (slow, deep, continental crust) and basalt (fast, oceanic floors) — the rock-cycle lesson\'s birth end, dated and chemically fingerprinted to their tectonic setting.</li>
    <li><strong>Geothermal energy rides the same heat:</strong> Iceland runs on volcanic heat (30% of electricity, ~90% of heating) — the energy lesson\'s quietest renewable.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Pliny the Younger\'s letters; Sigurdsson, Melting the Earth; USGS Volcano Hazards Program; the Pinatubo case (Newhall & Punongbayan, 1996).</p>
</TopicPage>
